# Rule Management and Organization

## Purpose
Guidelines for organizing, managing, and preventing duplication in cursor rules.

## Scope
This rule covers rule organization, cross-referencing, consolidation, and maintenance processes.

## Rule Metadata Requirements

### Required Metadata Header
Every cursor rule MUST start with the metadata header:

```markdown
---
title: "Rule Title"
description: "Brief description of what this rule covers"
version: "1.0.0"
category: "core|templates|plugins|addons|utilities|management"
tags: ["tag1", "tag2", "tag3"]
dependencies: ["../other-rule.mdc"]
---
```

### Metadata Fields

#### title (Required)
- **Type**: String
- **Description**: Clear, descriptive title for the rule
- **Example**: `title: "Metadata Structure Requirements"`

#### description (Required)
- **Type**: String
- **Description**: Brief description of what the rule covers
- **Example**: `description: "Defines the required metadata structure and format for SND macros"`

#### version (Required)
- **Type**: String
- **Description**: Version number using semantic versioning
- **Example**: `version: "1.0.0"`
- **Format**: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH

#### category (Required)
- **Type**: String
- **Description**: Category this rule belongs to
- **Options**: `core`, `templates`, `plugins`, `addons`, `utilities`, `management`
- **Example**: `category: "core"`

#### tags (Optional)
- **Type**: Array of strings
- **Description**: Tags for categorization and search
- **Example**: `tags: ["metadata", "structure", "validation"]`

#### dependencies (Optional)
- **Type**: Array of strings
- **Description**: Other rules this rule depends on
- **Example**: `dependencies: ["../core/error-handling.mdc"]`

#### alwaysApply (Optional)
- **Type**: Boolean
- **Description**: Whether this rule should always be loaded and active
- **Default**: `false`
- **Usage**:
  - `true`: Always loaded, applies to all files (higher memory usage)
  - `false`: Loaded on-demand when relevant files are opened (lower memory usage)
- **Recommendation**:
  - Use `true` for core development rules that should always be available
  - Use `false` for specialized plugin/addon rules that are only needed occasionally
- **Example**: `alwaysApply: true`

## Rule Duplication Prevention and Organization

### Core Principle
**No rule should be defined in multiple places.** Each rule should have a single source of truth and be properly referenced elsewhere.

### Rule Organization Structure

#### 1. Rule Categories
Organize rules into logical categories:

```
.cursor/rules/
├── core/                    # Core development rules
│   ├── metadata-structure.mdc        # Metadata structure requirements
│   ├── configuration-system.mdc      # Configuration system patterns
│   ├── version-management.mdc        # Version control and semantic versioning
│   ├── state-machines.mdc            # State machine architecture
│   ├── character-conditions.mdc      # Character condition checking
│   └── error-handling.mdc            # Error handling patterns
├── templates/               # Template and automation rules
│   └── lua-automation.mdc   # Lua file automation and templates
├── plugins/                 # Individual plugin integration rules
│   ├── vnavmesh.mdc         # vnavmesh navigation integration
│   ├── artisan.mdc          # Artisan crafting integration
│   ├── lifestream.mdc       # Lifestream teleportation integration
│   ├── saucy.mdc            # Saucy fishing integration
│   └── autoretainer-integration.mdc  # AutoRetainer plugin integration
├── addons/                  # Addon interaction rules
│   ├── crafting-addons.mdc  # Crafting addon interactions
│   ├── shop-addons.mdc      # Shop addon interactions
│   └── game-addons.mdc      # General game addon interactions
├── utilities/               # Utility and helper function rules
│   ├── distance-calculations.mdc     # Distance calculation utilities
│   ├── inventory-management.mdc      # Inventory management utilities
│   └── status-checking.mdc           # Status checking utilities
└── management/              # Rule management itself
    └── rule-management.mdc  # This file
```

#### 2. Rule Naming Convention
- Use descriptive, kebab-case names
- Include category prefix when appropriate
- Examples: `snd-development.mdc`, `lua-automation.mdc`, `plugin-integration.mdc`

### Rule Creation Process

#### Before Creating a New Rule
1. **Search existing rules** for similar content
2. **Check if rule already exists** in a different file
3. **Determine the appropriate category** for the new rule
4. **Plan the rule's scope** and boundaries

#### Rule Creation Checklist
- [ ] Rule doesn't duplicate existing content
- [ ] Rule has a clear, single responsibility
- [ ] Rule is placed in the correct category
- [ ] Rule has a descriptive filename
- [ ] Rule includes proper cross-references to related rules

### Rule Referencing System

#### 1. Cross-Reference Format
When referencing other rules, use this format:
```markdown
> **See also**: [Rule Name](relative-path-to-rule.mdc)
```

#### 2. Related Rules Section
Each rule should include a "Related Rules" section:
```markdown
## Related Rules
- [SND Development Guidelines](snd-development.mdc) - Core development patterns
- [Lua Metadata Template](templates/lua-metadata-template.mdc) - Metadata structure
- [Plugin Integration](integrations/plugin-integration.mdc) - Plugin usage patterns
```

#### 3. Rule Dependencies
Document which rules depend on others:
```markdown
## Rule Dependencies
This rule extends and depends on:
- [SND Development Guidelines](../core/snd-development.mdc)
- [Code Standards](../core/code-standards.mdc)
```

### Rule Consolidation Process

#### When Duplication is Found
1. **Identify the authoritative source** (usually the most comprehensive or recent)
2. **Move unique content** from duplicate to authoritative source
3. **Update all references** to point to the authoritative source
4. **Delete duplicate files**
5. **Update cross-references** in remaining rules

#### Consolidation Checklist
- [ ] All unique content preserved
- [ ] No information lost during consolidation
- [ ] All references updated
- [ ] Duplicate files removed
- [ ] Cross-references updated

### Rule Maintenance

#### Regular Review Process
1. **Monthly review** of all rules for:
   - Duplication
   - Outdated information
   - Missing cross-references
   - Inconsistent formatting

2. **Before major updates**:
   - Check for impact on other rules
   - Update related rules if necessary
   - Maintain cross-reference integrity

#### Rule Update Protocol
1. **Identify affected rules** before making changes
2. **Update cross-references** in related rules
3. **Test rule interactions** after updates
4. **Document changes** in rule changelog

### Rule Documentation Standards

#### Required Sections
Every rule file must include:
1. **Title and Purpose** - Clear description of what the rule covers
2. **Scope** - What is included/excluded
3. **Implementation** - How to follow the rule
4. **Examples** - Practical examples
5. **Related Rules** - Cross-references to other rules
6. **Rule Dependencies** - Which rules this depends on

#### Optional Sections
- **Exceptions** - When the rule doesn't apply
- **Best Practices** - Additional guidance
- **Common Mistakes** - What to avoid
- **Troubleshooting** - Common issues and solutions

### Rule Conflict Resolution

#### When Rules Conflict
1. **Identify the conflict** clearly
2. **Determine precedence** based on:
   - Rule specificity (more specific overrides general)
   - Rule recency (newer rules override older)
   - Rule authority (core rules override utility rules)
3. **Resolve the conflict** by:
   - Updating one rule to reference the other
   - Consolidating conflicting rules
   - Creating a hierarchy of rules
4. **Document the resolution** and update cross-references

### Rule Versioning

#### Version Tracking
- Use semantic versioning for major rule changes
- Include version history in rule files
- Track breaking changes and migrations

#### Example Version Section
```markdown
## Version History
- v1.0.0 - Initial rule creation
- v1.1.0 - Added plugin integration examples
- v2.0.0 - BREAKING: Restructured configuration system
```

### Rule Testing and Validation

#### Rule Validation Checklist
- [ ] Rule is clear and unambiguous
- [ ] Rule doesn't conflict with existing rules
- [ ] Rule has proper cross-references
- [ ] Rule follows naming conventions
- [ ] Rule is in the correct category
- [ ] Rule includes examples
- [ ] Rule is properly documented

#### Rule Testing Process
1. **Create test scenarios** based on the rule
2. **Verify rule behavior** in practice
3. **Check rule interactions** with other rules
4. **Validate cross-references** work correctly

### Emergency Rule Management

#### When Rules Break
1. **Immediate fix** - Address the immediate issue
2. **Root cause analysis** - Understand why the rule failed
3. **Rule update** - Fix the underlying rule
4. **Impact assessment** - Check for effects on other rules
5. **Documentation update** - Update related documentation

### Rule Archive and Cleanup

#### Archiving Obsolete Rules
- Move outdated rules to `.cursor/rules/archive/`
- Update all cross-references to point to replacement rules
- Document why the rule was archived
- Keep archive for reference but mark as deprecated

#### Cleanup Process
- Remove unused cross-references
- Consolidate similar rules
- Update rule documentation
- Verify all references still work

## Self-Improvement and Quality Assurance

### Rule Audit Process
Regular auditing of all rules to ensure compliance with rule management guidelines:

#### Monthly Rule Audit Checklist
- [ ] **Metadata Compliance**: All rules have proper metadata headers
- [ ] **Cross-Reference Integrity**: All "Related Rules" and "Rule Dependencies" sections are accurate
- [ ] **No Circular References**: Rules don't reference each other in circular patterns
- [ ] **Consistent Naming**: All rule names follow kebab-case convention
- [ ] **Proper Categorization**: Rules are in correct category directories
- [ ] **Dependency Accuracy**: Metadata dependencies match actual rule dependencies
- [ ] **Content Freshness**: All examples and patterns are current and accurate
- [ ] **No Duplication**: No content is duplicated across multiple rules

#### Automated Quality Checks
```markdown
## Rule Quality Validation

### Required Sections Check
Every rule MUST contain:
- [ ] Purpose section
- [ ] Scope section  
- [ ] Related Rules section
- [ ] Rule Dependencies section
- [ ] Proper metadata header

### Cross-Reference Validation
- [ ] All referenced rules exist
- [ ] All referenced rules are in correct directories
- [ ] No broken links in cross-references
- [ ] No circular dependencies

### Content Quality Checks
- [ ] Examples are complete and functional
- [ ] Code snippets are properly formatted
- [ ] Best practices are clearly stated
- [ ] Error handling is comprehensive
```

### Self-Improvement Triggers
Rules should be updated when:
1. **New patterns emerge** in the codebase
2. **Best practices change** based on experience
3. **New plugins or addons** are integrated
4. **User feedback** indicates improvements needed
5. **Rule violations** are discovered during audits

### Continuous Improvement Process
1. **Identify Issues**: Through regular audits or user feedback
2. **Analyze Root Cause**: Understand why the issue occurred
3. **Fix Immediate Problem**: Address the specific violation
4. **Update Prevention Rules**: Add guidelines to prevent recurrence
5. **Document Lessons Learned**: Update rule management guidelines
6. **Communicate Changes**: Update all affected rules and cross-references

### Quality Metrics
Track rule quality through:
- **Audit Completion Rate**: Percentage of rules passing monthly audits
- **Cross-Reference Accuracy**: Percentage of valid cross-references
- **User Satisfaction**: Feedback on rule usefulness and clarity
- **Maintenance Overhead**: Time spent fixing rule violations

